    "# ## 4. Build Training Dataset from Historical Tournament Games\n",
    "# \n",
    "# For each tournament game, we need a row with features for the two teams and the target (1 if first team wins, 0 otherwise). We'll always put the winning team as `Team1`? Actually, we want symmetric representation: we can keep the game as is and then compute features like `stat_team1 - stat_team2`. To avoid duplication, we'll create two rows per game? No, standard approach: for each game, we define Team1 as the lower TeamID (as required in submission) or simply keep as given. But the winner/loser columns are asymmetric. We'll create a new dataframe where each row represents a game with `Team1ID`, `Team2ID`, and `Target` where Target=1 if Team1 wins. We'll arbitrarily set Team1 = WTeamID, Team2 = LTeamID, Target=1. This is fine because the model will learn that the features correspond to Team1's stats minus Team2's stats. When predicting, we'll need to order teams so that the same feature computation (Team1 - Team2) is used.\n",
    "# \n",
    "# Later, when we predict for 2026, the sample submission IDs have Team1 < Team2, so we must ensure that for prediction we always put the smaller ID as Team1. So during training, we might also enforce Team1 < Team2? That would create two rows for each game (one with Team1=winner, Team2=loser and Target=1; and one with Team1=loser, Team2=winner and Target=0). That's a common technique to double the training data and teach the model symmetry. Let's do that: for each game, we create two rows: (TeamA=winner, TeamB=loser, target=1) and (TeamA=loser, TeamB=winner, target=0). This way the model learns that swapping teams flips the probability. Then during prediction, we always put the smaller ID first as required.\n",
    "# \n",
    "# We'll also merge in the seeds for each team in that tournament.\n",
    "\n",
    "# %%\n",
    "# Build training data from tournament compact results\n",
    "def build_training_data(tourney_df, seeds_df):\n",
    "    rows = []\n",
    "    for _, game in tourney_df.iterrows():\n",
    "        season = game['Season']\n",
    "        wteam = game['WTeamID']\n",
    "        lteam = game['LTeamID']\n",
    "        \n",
    "        # Row 1: Team1 = winner, Team2 = loser, target = 1\n",
    "        rows.append({\n",
    "            'Season': season,\n",
    "            'Team1ID': wteam,\n",
    "            'Team2ID': lteam,\n",
    "            'Target': 1\n",
    "        })\n",
    "        # Row 2: Team1 = loser, Team2 = winner, target = 0\n",
    "        rows.append({\n",
    "            'Season': season,\n",
    "            'Team1ID': lteam,\n",
    "            'Team2ID': wteam,\n",
    "            'Target': 0\n",
    "        })\n",
    "    train_df = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Merge seeds for Team1 and Team2\n",
    "    seeds_renamed = seeds_df.rename(columns={'TeamID': 'Team1ID', 'SeedNum': 'Seed1'})\n",
    "    train_df = train_df.merge(seeds_renamed[['Season', 'Team1ID', 'Seed1']], on=['Season', 'Team1ID'], how='left')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    seeds_renamed2 = seeds_df.rename(columns={'TeamID': 'Team2ID', 'SeedNum': 'Seed2'})\n",
    "    train_df = train_df.merge(seeds_renamed2[['Season', 'Team2ID', 'Seed2']], on=['Season', 'Team2ID'], how='left')\n",
    "    \n",
    "    # Compute seed difference\n",
    "    train_df['SeedDiff'] = train_df['Seed1'] - train_df['Seed2']\n",
    "    \n",
    "    return train_df\n",
